**Adversarial Robustness for VLAs:** Less Is More [1] (arXiv:2601.12042, 2025) — shows VLA compression is adversarially vulnerable, proposes adversarial training with \"only limited protection.\" CAGE [2] (ICML 2026) — formalizes attack surface of token compression but offers no defense. RobustVLA — Jacobian regularization for full model, not compression boundary. ProGAL-VLA — randomized smoothing for certified action robustness, not token selection.\n\n**Certified Robustness:** Randomized smoothing for ℓ2 classifiers. Interval bound propagation for neural networks. Paper claims first certified stability guarantee specifically for top-k selection in token compression.\n\n**Only 2 formal references** cited in the paper ([1] and [2]).\n\n### Method (Section 3)\n- **Core Insight**: Importance margin collapse (small Δ_k) makes top-k selection unstable under perturbations.\n- **Certified Bound**: P(top-k changes) ≤ k·ε·L/Δ_k for L-Lipschitz scorer with perturbation ‖δ‖ ≤ ε.\n- **IMM Loss**: L_IMM = L_action + λ·L_margin, where L_margin = max(0, γ - Δ_k) hinge loss with adaptive γ.\n- **Two-Stage Training**: Stage 1 freeze backbone → train ACIS; Stage 2 freeze ACIS → LoRA backbone. Eliminates co-adaptation.\n- **ACIS**: Cross-attention head + 2-layer MLP. 2.3M parameters.\n\n### Key Claims\n1. First certified robustness bound for VLA token compression (empirically tight at 1.51×)\n2. Two-stage protocol enables lossless compression (G3 < 1.0) with denoising effect\n3. Mechanistic explanation: pruned tokens are background noise (83% background, 0.25× attention, 0.14× gradient)\n\n### Results Summary\n- aloha_sim_insertion: 88% vs 82% baseline (+6pp, p=0.031)\n- LIBERO 3 suites: +4.7pp average, all G3 < 1.0\n- OpenVLA 7B validation: +3.7pp, all G3 < 1.0\n- Defense comparison: IMM Pareto-dominant (76% clean, 71% perturbed vs AT 68/63, RS 70/61, JR 72/65)\n- Cross-compression: +48-52pp preservation on FastV/VLA-IAP/VLA-Cache\n- ACIS overhead: +0.9% params, -36.5% latency, -42%