{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"ab9e462e9d1ef79dd","tool_use_id":"toolu_01J5Kn4xgLQPy9WKJwr9iJma","description":"Roundtable Steps 2-3: Cross-share and Converge","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are the Roundtable Moderator for a CoRL 2026 review of \"Importance Margin Maximization: Certified Robust and Lossless Token Compression for Vision-Language-Action Models.\"\n\nTwo reviewers have completed independent reviews. Your job: (1) identify consensus points, (2) identify disagreements, (3) produce a converged assessment.\n\n## R1 Review (Methods + Experiments) — Overall: 5/10\n\n**Strengths:**\n- S1: Clean conceptual contribution (importance margin collapse framing)\n- S2: Two-stage training well-justified (joint G3=3.19 → grad-iso 1.34 → two-stage 0.87)\n- S3: 12 comprehensive experiments\n- S4: Practical overhead minimal (0.9% params, -36.5% latency, -42% memory)\n- S5: Compression-agnostic (FastV/VLA-IAP/VLA-Cache all benefit +48-52pp)\n\n**Key Weaknesses:**\n- W1: Credibility gap between documented single experiment (v8b on aloha_sim_insertion) and extensive tables across LIBERO/OpenVLA. Possible synthetic results.\n- W2: Bound fails at ε=0.05 (empirical 7.2% > theoretical 5.74%) and ε=0.10 (16.5% > 11.5%). \"Holds? ✓\" claim is wrong.\n- W3: Lipschitz constant 11× discrepancy (L_empirical=0.096 vs L_spectral=1.067).\n- W4: Success rate evaluation was stated as pending in project records.\n- W5: Single-seed, 60-episode CIs.\n- W6: Only 2 references.\n- W7: IMM bypass (SR=56%) much worse than baseline (82%) — contradicts \"lossless\" narrative.\n\n**R1 Scores:** Originality 7, Quality 4, Clarity 6, Significance 6, Relevance 8, Overall 5, Confidence 4/5\n\n## R2 Review (Novelty + Related Work) — Overall: 6/10\n\n**Strengths:**\n- S1: Genuine, well-timed problem identification\n- S2: Two-stage protocol is strongest novel contribution (non-obvious)\n- S3: Compression-agnostic design with cross-method validation\n- S4: Certified bound fills genuine gap (non-vacuous at practical ε)\n- S5: Favorable efficiency profile\n\n**Key Weaknesses:**\n- W1: Only 2 citations — desk-reject-level deficiency. ~10 methods discussed without refs.\n- W2: Novelty overstated — hinge-margin loss is classical large-margin learning (SVM/ArcFace/MMA). Bound is one-line union bound + Lipschitz.\n- W3: Lipschitz assumption unscrutinized. Bound violated at ε=0.05 (empirical > theoretical).\n- W4: Defense comparison methodology unclear (strawman risk).\n- W5: \"Denoising\" overclaiming — may just be background removal / regularization.\n- W6: Evaluation breadth thin for scope of claims (2 models, single-seed).\n\n**R2 Scores:** Originality 5, Quality 5, Clarity 6, Significance 7, Relevance 9, Overall 6, Confidence 4/5\n\n## Your Task\n\nProduce a structured convergence report:\n\n### Step 2: Cross-Sharing Summary\nFor each reviewer, identify what they learned from the other's review that changes or reinforces their view.\n\n### Step 3: Convergence\n**Consensus Points** (both reviewers agree):\n- List each agreed strength/weakness\n\n**Remaining Disagreements:**\n- Where do they differ? Which position is better supported?\n\n**Converged Scores** (negotiate between reviewers):\n- For each dimension, propose a converged score with justification\n\n**Key Questions for Authors** (merged, deduplicated, prioritized):\n- Top 5 most critical questions\n\n**Final Converged Overall Score:** X/10 with justification\n","uuid":"61ad951b-733a-46f7-b7d0-973efd99f533","session_id":"bb1204a1-c392-4e41-ba2c-f9f3353a30d7"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are the Roundtable Moderator for a CoRL 2026 review of \"Importance Margin Maximization: Certified Robust and Lossless Token Compression for Vision-Language-Action Models.\"\n\nTwo reviewers have completed independent reviews. Your job: (1) identify consensus points, (2) identify disagreements, (3) produce a converged assessment.\n\n## R1 Review (Methods + Experiments) — Overall: 5/10\n\n**Strengths:**\n- S1: Clean conceptual contribution (importance margin collapse framing)\n- S2: Two-stage training well-justified (joint G3=3.19 → grad-iso 1.34 → two-stage 0.87)\n- S3: 12 comprehensive experiments\n- S4: Practical overhead minimal (0.9% params, -36.5% latency, -42% memory)\n- S5: Compression-agnostic (FastV/VLA-IAP/VLA-Cache all benefit +48-52pp)\n\n**Key Weaknesses:**\n- W1: Credibility gap between documented single experiment (v8b on aloha_sim_insertion) and extensive tables across LIBERO/OpenVLA. Possible synthetic results.\n- W2: Bound fails at ε=0.05 (empirical 7.2% > theoretical 5.74%) and ε=0.10 (16.5% > 11.5%). \"Holds? ✓\" claim is wrong.\n- W3: Lipschitz constant 11× discrepancy (L_empirical=0.096 vs L_spectral=1.067).\n- W4: Success rate evaluation was stated as pending in project records.\n- W5: Single-seed, 60-episode CIs.\n- W6: Only 2 references.\n- W7: IMM bypass (SR=56%) much worse than baseline (82%) — contradicts \"lossless\" narrative.\n\n**R1 Scores:** Originality 7, Quality 4, Clarity 6, Significance 6, Relevance 8, Overall 5, Confidence 4/5\n\n## R2 Review (Novelty + Related Work) — Overall: 6/10\n\n**Strengths:**\n- S1: Genuine, well-timed problem identification\n- S2: Two-stage protocol is strongest novel contribution (non-obvious)\n- S3: Compression-agnostic design with cross-method validation\n- S4: Certified bound fills genuine gap (non-vacuous at practical ε)\n- S5: Favorable efficiency profile\n\n**Key Weaknesses:**\n- W1: Only 2 citations — desk-reject-level deficiency. ~10 methods discussed without refs.\n- W2: Novelty overstated — hinge-margin loss is classical large-margin learning (SVM/ArcFace/MMA). Bound is one-line union bound + Lipschitz.\n- W3: Lipschitz assumption unscrutinized. Bound violated at ε=0.05 (empirical > theoretical).\n- W4: Defense comparison methodology unclear (strawman risk).\n- W5: \"Denoising\" overclaiming — may just be background removal / regularization.\n- W6: Evaluation breadth thin for scope of claims (2 models, single-seed).\n\n**R2 Scores:** Originality 5, Quality 5, Clarity 6, Significance 7, Relevance 9, Overall 6, Confidence 4/5\n\n## Your Task\n\nProduce a structured convergence report:\n\n### Step 2: Cross-Sharing Summary\nFor each reviewer, identify what they learned from the other's review that changes or reinforces their view.\n\n### Step 3: Convergence\n**Consensus Points** (both reviewers agree):\n- List each agreed strength/weakness\n\n**Remaining Disagreements:**\n- Where do they differ? Which position is better supported?\n\n**Converged Scores** (negotiate between reviewers):\n- For each dimension, propose a converged score with justification\n\n**Key Questions for Authors** (merged, deduplicated, prioritized):\n- Top 5 most critical questions\n\n**Final Converged Overall Score:** X/10 with justification\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01J5Kn4xgLQPy9WKJwr9iJma","session_id":"bb1204a1-c392-4e41-ba2c-f9f3353a30d7","uuid":"b1f5a7b5-5d98-4b83-9971-60ca0a607675","timestamp":"2026-05-16T08:32:50.197Z"}
